id [&amp;_&gt;_*]:min-w-0 whitespace-normal break-words print:block"><h2 di=
r=3D"ltr" aria-level=3D"4">Review of <code dir=3D"ltr">GetOrdersWithTotalAs=
ync</code></h2>
<h3 dir=3D"ltr" aria-level=3D"5">Assumptions</h3>
<p dir=3D"ltr">Since only this method was shared, I=E2=80=99m assuming the =
following. If any are wrong, the severity of some issues changes.</p>
<ul dir=3D"ltr">
<li><code dir=3D"ltr">_context</code> is an EF Core <code dir=3D"ltr">DbCon=
text</code> (the code uses <code dir=3D"ltr">ToListAsync</code>, so EF Core=
 is the only reasonable fit).</li>
<li><code dir=3D"ltr">Order</code> has a navigation property <code dir=3D"l=
tr">Items</code> (a collection of line items with <code dir=3D"ltr">Price</=
code> and <code dir=3D"ltr">Quantity</code>), and <code dir=3D"ltr">Total</=
code> is a settable property on <code dir=3D"ltr">Order</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Lazy loading is either enabled or <code dir=3D"ltr">Items</code=
> is not loaded elsewhere.</strong> This is the most important unknown, bec=
ause it decides which failure mode you hit (see issue 1).</li>
<li>No global query filters, no <code dir=3D"ltr">AsNoTracking</code> defau=
lt, and no pagination are configured on the context. I haven=E2=80=99t assu=
med any extra configuration.</li>
<li><code dir=3D"ltr">Total</code> is not mapped to a database column, or i=
f it is, it=E2=80=99s intentionally recomputed here.</li>
</ul>
<h3 dir=3D"ltr" aria-level=3D"5">Performance issues</h3>
<h4 dir=3D"ltr" aria-level=3D"6">1. The <code dir=3D"ltr">Items</code> acce=
ss is broken or an N+1 (the critical one)</h4>
<p dir=3D"ltr"><code dir=3D"ltr">ToListAsync()</code> on <code dir=3D"ltr">=
_context.Orders</code> does not load <code dir=3D"ltr">Items</code> unless =
something else makes it load. Two outcomes:</p>
<ul dir=3D"ltr">
<li><strong>Lazy loading proxies enabled:</strong> every <code dir=3D"ltr">=
order.Items</code> access triggers a separate synchronous query. For N orde=
rs you get 1 + N queries. Worse, lazy loading is synchronous, so it blocks =
a thread inside an <code dir=3D"ltr">async</code> method.</li>
<li><strong>Lazy loading not enabled (EF Core default):</strong> <code dir=
=3D"ltr">order.Items</code> is empty (or null), so every <code dir=3D"ltr">=
Total</code> silently becomes <code dir=3D"ltr">0</code>. No exception, jus=
t wrong data. In production this is arguably worse than the slow version.</=
li>
</ul>
<p dir=3D"ltr">Either way, the code doesn=E2=80=99t reliably do what its na=
me says.</p>
<h4 dir=3D"ltr" aria-level=3D"6">2. Unbounded query: loads the entire table=
</h4>
<p dir=3D"ltr">There is no <code dir=3D"ltr">Where</code>, no <code dir=3D"=
ltr">Take</code>, no paging. As the <code dir=3D"ltr">Orders</code> table g=
rows, this loads every row into memory. Memory use, latency and GC pressure=
 grow linearly with table size, and a single large call can degrade the who=
le service.</p>
<h4 dir=3D"ltr" aria-level=3D"6">3. Change tracking overhead for read-only =
data</h4>
<p dir=3D"ltr">Entities are tracked by default. For a read-only listing, th=
e change tracker allocates snapshots and identity-resolution structures for=
 every entity, and gains nothing. It=E2=80=99s worse because the code then =
mutates <code dir=3D"ltr">order.Total</code>, which marks entities as modif=
ied, so a later <code dir=3D"ltr">SaveChanges</code> on the same context co=
uld write unintended updates.</p>
<h4 dir=3D"ltr" aria-level=3D"6">4. Aggregation is done in memory instead o=
f the database</h4>
<p dir=3D"ltr">Even if <code dir=3D"ltr">Items</code> were loaded with <cod=
e dir=3D"ltr">Include</code>, you=E2=80=99d transfer every item row over th=
e wire just to sum them. The database can compute the total and return one =
number per order.</p>
<h4 dir=3D"ltr" aria-level=3D"6">5. Loading whole entities when you need a =
subset</h4>
<p dir=3D"ltr">The method pulls every column of every order. A listing usua=
lly needs only a handful of fields.</p>
<h3 dir=3D"ltr" aria-level=3D"5">Architectural issues</h3>
<h4 aria-level=3D"6" dir=3D"ltr">6. Returning EF entities from a data-acces=
s method</h4>
<p dir=3D"ltr">Returning <code dir=3D"ltr">List&lt;Order&gt;</code> exposes=
 tracked entity types to callers. This couples consumers to the persistence=
 model, risks accidental lazy loads or serialization cycles later, and mean=
s the API contract changes whenever the schema does. A DTO or projection ty=
pe is the safer boundary.</p>
<h4 dir=3D"ltr" aria-level=3D"6">7. Mutating an entity with a computed valu=
e</h4>
<p dir=3D"ltr"><code dir=3D"ltr">Total</code> is derived data, but the code=
 writes it onto the entity. This creates ambiguity about whether <code dir=
=3D"ltr">Total</code> is persisted or calculated, and the mutation is exact=
ly what makes issue 3 dangerous. Derived values belong in a projection, not=
 on a tracked entity.</p>
<h4 dir=3D"ltr" aria-level=3D"6">8. No cancellation support</h4>
<p dir=3D"ltr">The method takes no <code dir=3D"ltr">CancellationToken</cod=
e>. If the client disconnects or a request times out, the database query ke=
eps running and holds a connection. In ASP.NET Core you=E2=80=99d normally =
pass <code dir=3D"ltr">HttpContext.RequestAborted</code> down the chain.</p=
>
<h4 dir=3D"ltr" aria-level=3D"6">9. Mixed responsibilities and weak naming/=
contract</h4>
<p dir=3D"ltr">The method fetches, calculates and shapes data in one place.=
 The name <code dir=3D"ltr">GetOrdersWithTotalAsync</code> also implies fil=
tering or scope that doesn=E2=80=99t exist. Returning <code dir=3D"ltr">Lis=
t&lt;T&gt;</code> rather than <code dir=3D"ltr">IReadOnlyList&lt;T&gt;</cod=
e> also lets callers mutate the result.</p>
<h4 dir=3D"ltr" aria-level=3D"6">10. Possible numeric correctness issue (as=
sumption-dependent)</h4>
<p dir=3D"ltr"><code dir=3D"ltr">i.Price * i.Quantity</code> summed in memo=
ry is fine for <code dir=3D"ltr">decimal</code>. If <code dir=3D"ltr">Price=
</code> is <code dir=3D"ltr">double</code>/<code dir=3D"ltr">float</code>, =
you=E2=80=99ll have rounding drift for money. Worth confirming the types, s=
ince I can=E2=80=99t see them.</p>
<h3 dir=3D"ltr" aria-level=3D"5">Suggested direction</h3>
<p dir=3D"ltr">This uses only standard EF Core APIs: <code dir=3D"ltr">AsNo=
Tracking</code>, <code dir=3D"ltr">Select</code>, <code dir=3D"ltr">Sum</co=
de>, <code dir=3D"ltr">Skip</code>/<code dir=3D"ltr">Take</code>, <code dir=
=3D"ltr">OrderBy</code>, and <code dir=3D"ltr">CancellationToken</code>. Pr=
operty names like <code dir=3D"ltr">Id</code> and <code dir=3D"ltr">OrderDa=
te</code> are my assumptions about your model.</p>
